---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CsvToExcel from '@/components/tools/data/CsvToExcel.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="csv-to-excel">
  <CsvToExcel client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert CSV to an Excel workbook</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste the CSV or drop a <code>.csv</code> / <code>.tsv</code> file onto the left pane.</li>
      <li>Confirm the delimiter. Auto-detect handles commas, semicolons, tabs and pipes, and the preview shows the grid immediately.</li>
      <li>Name the sheet and choose if the first row is a header.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Download .xlsx</strong>. Your browser writes the workbook and saves it to your downloads folder.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why not just rename the file to .xlsx?</h2>
    <p>
      <code>.xlsx</code> is not a text format. It is a ZIP archive with XML parts that describe sheets,
      shared strings, styles and relationships. A renamed CSV does not open in Excel, or opens with a
      “the file format and extension don’t match” warning. This tool writes a real workbook with
      SheetJS, so Excel, LibreOffice Calc, Numbers and Google Sheets open it without warnings.
    </p>

    <h2>Problems this solves that a raw CSV does not</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-max border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">CSV problem</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">What the workbook does instead</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Everything lands in column A</td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Columns are defined in the file, so the local list separator does not matter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Accents turn into mojibake</td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">XLSX is UTF-8 internally; no BOM guesswork</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Every column is the same narrow width</td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Widths are measured from the longest value and written into the file</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Numbers arrive as text and will not sum</td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Numeric cells are written with a numeric type</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">No way to filter or sort on open</td>
            <td class="border-b px-3 py-2">An autofilter is added to the header row</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Keeping leading zeros and long identifiers</h2>
    <p>
      This is the classic spreadsheet data loss: postcodes such as <code>01234</code> become
      <code>1234</code>, and a 16-digit account number becomes <code>4.91634E+15</code>. Turn off
      <strong>Convert numbers and booleans</strong> to write each cell as text, so those values stay
      exactly as typed. With the option on, the converter still does not convert values with a leading
      zero or more than fifteen digits to numbers, because those are almost always identifiers, not
      quantities.
    </p>

    <h2>Sheet naming rules</h2>
    <p>
      Excel allows at most 31 characters in a sheet name and forbids the characters
      <code>: \ / ? * [ ]</code>. The tool replaces invalid characters with a space and truncates the
      name before it writes the file, so Excel always opens the workbook.
    </p>

    <h2>Delimiters and quoting</h2>
    <p>
      Parsing follows RFC 4180: a quoted field can contain the delimiter, line breaks and doubled quotes.
      Multi-line addresses in quotes thus stay in one cell and do not spill onto new rows, a common
      failure of simple CSV splitters.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing leaves your device</h2>
    <p>
      The CSV is parsed and the workbook is built in this browser tab, then passed to the browser as an
      in-memory blob. There is no upload, so contact lists, invoices and export dumps stay on your
      device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
